Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:  

- Relationship between father and newborn: A good or very good relationship likely reduces risk of postpartum depression (PPD), while bad or neutral may increase risk.  
- Family type: Joint family may provide more support and reduce PPD risk; nuclear family may have less support and higher risk.  
- Received Support: Higher support levels reduce PPD risk; low support increases risk.  
- History of pregnancy loss: Having a history of miscarriage or still-born delivery may increase risk of PPD due to trauma and anxiety.  
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy: Higher income may reduce stress and risk of PPD; very low income may increase risk.  
- Number of the latest pregnancy: Higher number of pregnancies may increase stress and risk; first pregnancy might have different stress dynamics.  
- Birth compliancy: Complications during birth may increase risk of PPD due to physical and emotional trauma.  
- Age of immediate older children: Having very young children (1yr to 6yr) may increase stress and risk of PPD; no older children or older children (>13yr) may reduce risk.  
- Marital status: Married status may provide emotional support reducing risk; divorced status may increase risk.  
- Occupation After Your Latest Childbirth: Being a housewife or student may increase isolation and risk; working in service, doctor, teacher, business may provide social interaction reducing risk.  

