Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Number of the latest pregnancy: First-time mothers may have higher risk of postpartum depression due to inexperience and anxiety.
- Age: Younger or older maternal age may influence risk; very young or older mothers might have higher risk.
- Relax/sleep when newborn is tended: If the patient can relax/sleep, less likely to have postpartum depression.
- Angry after latest child birth: Feeling angry may be a symptom or indicator of postpartum depression.
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy: Lower income may increase stress and risk of depression.
- Mode of delivery: Caesarean Section may be associated with higher postpartum depression risk due to surgery stress.
- Marital status: Divorced or single mothers may have higher risk due to less social support.
- Gender of newborn: Some cultures have gender preference that may affect mother's mood.
- Fear of pregnancy: Fear or anxiety related to pregnancy can increase risk of postpartum depression.
- Husband's education level: Higher education may correlate with better support and lower risk.

